---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import Outliner from "../components/Outliner";
---

<ToolPage tool="contour">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Trace the <strong class="text-slate-300">cut line around a design</strong> — for the backing plate you
        glue the original on top of. The geometry comes from the very same extraction the DXF export uses, so
        the outline is traced around exactly what would be cut.
    </Fragment>

    <Outliner client:load />

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto grid w-[min(72rem,100%)] gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
            <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Items, not shapes</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Every subpath is treated as a ring, and a ring is an <strong class="text-slate-300">item</strong> when no other ring contains it. Holes and inner detail therefore drop out by themselves, and
                    things standing side by side come out as separate items — trace all of them, or switch to <em>individual</em> and click the ones you want.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Border 0 mm is exact</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    At zero the item's outermost path <em>is</em> the cut line, to the 0.01 mm the curve flattener
                    works at. Give it a border and the offset is computed on a fine grid instead — the accuracy it
                    reports, typically 0.03 mm — because offsetting a polygon outwards means resolving the
                    self-intersections it creates at every concave corner.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Three ways to join</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">Shrink-wrap</strong> closes the mask with the reach the gaps
                    need, so one smooth outline sweeps from item to item and hugs each. <strong class="text-slate-300">Bridges</strong> adds a 4 mm neck along the shortest route,
                    filleted where it meets an item. <strong class="text-slate-300">Taut band</strong> is the
                    convex hull. The automatic reach opens up until the selection really is one piece.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
